1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:12,499
In the summer of 449, a Roman diplomat named Priscus rode north across the Danube into the lands of the Huns, to meet the most feared man in Europe.

2
00:00:13,599 --> 00:00:25,375
He found him at a feast in a wooden hall. The guests ate from silver plates and drank from gold cups. Their host, Attila, used a plain wooden cup and wore simple clothes.

3
00:00:26,475 --> 00:00:34,880
By then, the Eastern Roman Empire was paying him two thousand one hundred pounds of gold every year, simply to be left alone.

4
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:47,479
Later Christian writers called him the Scourge of God, a punishment sent from heaven. Yet he ruled for less than twenty years, and his empire vanished almost as soon as he died.

5
00:00:49,679 --> 00:00:59,535
The Huns were nomadic horsemen from the steppes of central Asia. Exactly where they came from is still debated. They were superb mounted archers.

6
00:01:00,635 --> 00:01:14,075
Around 370, they burst into eastern Europe, driving the Goths before them. Fleeing Goths crossed into the Roman Empire, setting off a chain of events that shook Rome to its foundations.

7
00:01:15,175 --> 00:01:26,097
By the 420s, the Huns had settled on the great plain of what is now Hungary. Rome sometimes fought them, and sometimes hired them as mercenaries.

8
00:01:27,197 --> 00:01:36,563
Attila was probably born around 406. Almost nothing is known of his childhood. His uncle, Rugila, ruled the Huns.

9
00:01:38,763 --> 00:01:47,083
When Rugila died, around 434, Attila and his elder brother, Bleda, inherited his kingdom together.

10
00:01:48,183 --> 00:01:58,977
They quickly forced the Eastern Roman Empire, ruled from Constantinople, into a treaty that doubled its yearly payment to them, to seven hundred pounds of gold.

11
00:02:00,077 --> 00:02:09,507
When the Romans fell behind, the brothers invaded the Balkans. Cities such as Naissus, the modern Niš in Serbia, were stormed and sacked.

12
00:02:10,607 --> 00:02:18,948
The peace that followed tripled the tribute to two thousand one hundred pounds of gold a year, plus six thousand pounds in arrears.

13
00:02:20,048 --> 00:02:28,133
Around 445, Bleda died. Roman writers say Attila had him killed. From then on, he ruled alone.

14
00:02:30,333 --> 00:02:38,909
Priscus described him as short and broad-chested, with a large head, small deep-set eyes, and a thin beard flecked with grey.

15
00:02:40,009 --> 00:02:49,951
Priscus watched him sit outside his hall, judging disputes brought to him by his people. His followers treated him with a mixture of devotion and fear.

16
00:02:51,051 --> 00:03:03,296
His empire was not only Huns. It included Germanic peoples such as the Ostrogoths and Gepids, and many others, held together by loyalty, fear and a share of the plunder.

17
00:03:04,396 --> 00:03:17,751
In 447, he struck the Balkans again, reaching as far south as Thermopylae in Greece. Constantinople's great walls, hastily repaired after an earthquake, kept him out of the capital.

18
00:03:18,851 --> 00:03:32,973
The Romans tried another way. While Priscus's embassy was at the Hun court, a senior Roman official was secretly plotting to have Attila assassinated. Attila found out, and sent envoys to shame the emperor.

19
00:03:35,173 --> 00:03:49,595
Then, in 450, came an extraordinary message. Honoria, sister of the Western Roman emperor, Valentinian, was being pushed into a marriage she did not want. She sent Attila her ring and a plea for help.

20
00:03:50,695 --> 00:03:59,804
Attila chose to treat it as a marriage proposal. He demanded Honoria as his bride, and half of the Western Empire as her dowry.

21
00:04:00,904 --> 00:04:08,008
Valentinian was furious. Honoria was married off to someone else, and Attila had his excuse for war.

22
00:04:09,108 --> 00:04:19,519
In 451, he crossed the Rhine into Gaul, modern France, with a huge army. Cities such as Metz were sacked, and Orléans was besieged.

23
00:04:20,619 --> 00:04:31,371
Against him stood Flavius Aetius, the Western Empire's leading general. As a young man, Aetius had lived among the Huns as a hostage, and knew them well.

24
00:04:32,471 --> 00:04:45,121
Aetius allied with the Visigoths, a Germanic people settled in southern Gaul. In the summer of 451, the two sides met at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains.

25
00:04:46,221 --> 00:04:59,000
It was one of the bloodiest battles of the age, and the Visigothic king, Theodoric, was killed. A later historian claimed a hundred and sixty-five thousand died, surely an exaggeration.

26
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:09,764
Attila was forced to retreat, but his army survived. Aetius let him go, perhaps wanting the Huns to remain as a counterweight to the Visigoths.

27
00:05:11,964 --> 00:05:23,228
The next year, he invaded Italy. After a long siege, the city of Aquileia, near the head of the Adriatic, was destroyed so thoroughly that it never fully recovered.

28
00:05:24,328 --> 00:05:32,008
Refugees fled to the islands of the nearby lagoons. Later tradition linked them to the founding of Venice.

29
00:05:33,108 --> 00:05:42,836
Milan and other cities fell. Then an embassy from Rome came to meet him, including Pope Leo the First. Soon afterwards, Attila turned back.

30
00:05:43,936 --> 00:05:54,603
Christian legend credited the pope. Historians also point to famine in Italy, disease in the Hun army, and an Eastern Roman attack on the Huns' homeland.

31
00:05:56,803 --> 00:06:06,232
Early in 453, Attila took another wife, a young woman named Ildico. After the wedding feast, he went to bed drunk.

32
00:06:07,332 --> 00:06:13,583
The next day, his attendants broke down the door and found him dead, his bride weeping beside him.

33
00:06:14,683 --> 00:06:25,477
According to Priscus, as retold by the Gothic historian Jordanes, he had suffered a severe nosebleed in his sleep and choked. Later rumours blamed his bride.

34
00:06:26,577 --> 00:06:39,185
Jordanes adds that he was buried at night in three coffins, of gold, silver and iron, and that the men who buried him were killed to keep the place secret. His grave has never been found.

35
00:06:41,385 --> 00:06:53,588
His sons fought over his empire. In 454, the subject peoples, led by Ardaric, king of the Gepids, rose up and defeated the Huns at the Battle of Nedao.

36
00:06:54,688 --> 00:07:07,445
Aetius did not outlive Attila by long. In 454, the emperor Valentinian killed him in the palace. Twenty-two years later, the Western Roman Empire itself was gone.

37
00:07:08,545 --> 00:07:18,359
One of his sons, Dengizich, kept fighting the Romans. In 469 he was killed, and his head was displayed in Constantinople.

38
00:07:19,459 --> 00:07:24,643
Within about fifteen years of Attila's death, the Hunnic empire had disappeared.

39
00:07:26,843 --> 00:07:38,277
Attila lived on in legend. In the German epic, the Nibelungenlied, he appears as Etzel, a fairly mild king. In Norse sagas, he is Atli, a greedy tyrant.

40
00:07:39,377 --> 00:07:46,823
In Hungary, where his empire was centred, he is remembered more kindly, and Attila is still a common boy's name.

41
00:07:47,923 --> 00:08:02,792
He left no buildings, laws or writings. Everything we know comes from enemies who feared him. Modern historians see a shrewd ruler who understood Rome's weaknesses, and preferred extorting gold to conquering land.

42
00:08:03,892 --> 00:08:15,433
Attila built nothing that lasted. His empire was a machine for taking gold and plunder, and it fell apart within a generation. What survived was the fear, and his name.
